# Mutation plan for the 2026-09-07 /data_retention group — fifteen routes.
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/data-retention.plan
#
# Every route answers 501 on this deployment, so the whole risk is in the checks **ahead** of the
# refusal, and they are in a different order in almost every handler:
#
#   * the body is decoded **first** on the five routes that take one, so a malformed body is a 400
#     even for a caller with no permission;
#   * the two body shapes produce two **different** error ids — `invalid_body_param` for a policy,
#     `api.payload.parse.error` for an id list — and a JSON `null` id list is not an error at all;
#   * `RequirePolicyId`'s result is **thrown away** on eleven routes, so a malformed policy id is a
#     501 and not a 400. Reproducing the check would be the bug;
#   * the two per-user routes are the exception: their id check is live, and their gate is
#     self-or-`manage_system`, not a sysconsole read;
#   * `getGlobalPolicy` has no permission check at all.
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.
dataret-body-decoded-after-the-permission	crates/mm-api/src/data_retention.rs	    if route.body_first\n        && let Err(err) = decode(route.body, &bytes)	    if !route.body_first\n        && let Err(err) = decode(route.body, &bytes)	api	data_retention
